From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fbtp: mark the baseboard as Inventory.Item.System

bmcweb's getComputerSystem() subtrees /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory looking
for xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item.System, and only for an object carrying
that interface does it read Inventory.Decorator.Asset (afterGetInventory) and
then, at the tail of that same callback, populate BiosVersion via
sw_util::populateSoftwareInformation().

TiogaPass had no object implementing Item.System at all -- only Decorator.Asset
objects (TiogaPass_Baseboard, cpu0/1, dimm0-11) -- so afterGetInventory() never
ran and /redfish/v1/Systems/system reported Manufacturer, Model, SerialNumber,
PartNumber, SubModel *and* BiosVersion all as null together.

The baseboard already carries Decorator.Asset sourced from the FRU, and
bmcweb reads Asset from the same object and service that exposes Item.System,
so tagging the existing board is sufficient -- no new object or daemon.
global.json permits Item.System as a board-level property (unlike
Decorator.Ipmi, which the schema strips), so entity-manager will publish it.

--- a/configurations/fbtp.json
+++ b/configurations/fbtp.json
@@ -1527,5 +1527,6 @@
     "Model": "$PRODUCT_PRODUCT_NAME",
     "PartNumber": "$PRODUCT_PART_NUMBER",
     "SerialNumber": "$PRODUCT_SERIAL_NUMBER"
-  }
+  },
+  "xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item.System": {}
 }
\ No newline at end of file
